var e=[{id:`po1`,code:`PO1`,title:`Helping children play, develop and learn in the early years`,gist:`PO1 is the heart of Early Years Educator practice: using play as the engine of development across the seven EYFS areas, while noticing how children learn (the characteristics of effective teaching and learning) as well as what they learn. You plan a mix of child-initiated and adult-led experiences, recognise schemas and play types, adapt for inclusion, and use observation so that next steps are real. Non-statutory guidance such as Development Matters and Birth to 5 Matters can shape a curriculum; the statutory EYFS is what you must meet.`,mustKnow:[`The seven EYFS areas: three prime (communication and language; physical development; personal, social and emotional development) and four specific (literacy; mathematics; understanding the world; expressive arts and design).`,`The three characteristics of effective teaching and learning: playing and exploring, active learning, and creating and thinking critically.`,`Play types include heuristic play, pretend and socio-dramatic play, constructive play, physical play, and outdoor or Forest School play. Each offers different learning if the environment is resourced and the adult role is right.`,`A balance of adult-led and child-initiated play is a curriculum requirement, not a preference. Adult-led teaching should still leave room for children's ideas.`,`Sustained shared thinking is a high-quality interaction: the adult and child think together, extending an idea over time, rather than firing closed questions.`,`Schemas are repeated play patterns (trajectory, rotation, enclosing, enveloping, transporting, connecting, positioning). Feed the schema with resources; do not only stop it as mess.`,`Observation informs next steps. A next step is small, play-based and reviewable, not a vague wish to 'improve development'.`,`Inclusive play means every child can access the experience: SEND adaptations, dual-language support, and anti-discriminatory resources are part of PO1, not an optional extra.`,`Development Matters and Birth to 5 Matters are non-statutory. They help you interpret typical development; they are not tick-lists and they do not replace the EYFS.`,`Pedagogical approaches in the specification include Moyles (spiral of play), Goldschmied (heuristic play), Bruce (free-flow play), Montessori (self-directed activity), Forest School (risk and challenge), HighScope (active learning), McMillan (outdoor play) and Steiner (creativity and expression).`],sections:[{heading:`The seven areas and how children learn`,body:[`Prime areas are foundational. A two-year-old whose language or PSED is insecure will not 'catch up' in literacy by being drilled on letters. Weight planning towards communication, physical development and PSED for the youngest children, and keep those areas visible in Reception too.`,`Specific areas grow out of the prime areas. Mathematics in the EYFS is counting, pattern, shape and spatial thinking in real contexts (snack, blocks, outdoors), not a worksheet culture. Literacy is talk, rhyme, print in the environment, sharing books and, later, systematic synthetic phonics.`,`The characteristics of effective teaching and learning cut across every area. Playing and exploring is about finding out and being willing to have a go. Active learning is concentration, persistence and taking pride in meeting a challenge. Creating and thinking critically is having ideas, making links and choosing ways to do things. Observe these as carefully as you observe ELG-style skills.`]},{heading:`Play types and the adult role`,body:[`Heuristic play (Goldschmied) offers treasure-basket or heuristic collections for babies and toddlers: metal, wood, fabric, natural objects, containers. The adult prepares, sits nearby and does not demonstrate a 'right' use. Concentration can last far longer than in an adult-directed craft.`,`Pretend and socio-dramatic play lets children rehearse roles, language and social rules. Resource it with open-ended props (fabric, bags, real or realistic utensils) and be ready to take a role without hijacking the plot. Constructive play (blocks, loose parts) builds spatial thinking, mathematics and collaboration. Physical play, indoors and out, builds the motor foundations for later writing and self-care.`,`Outdoor and Forest School play offers weather, uneven ground, real tools and managed risk. Margaret McMillan's argument still holds: outdoor play is for health and learning, not a fair-weather extra. Adult-led experiences (a planned phonics group, a cooking sequence) sit alongside child-initiated play. The skill is knowing when to step in with a prompt and when to step back.`]},{heading:`Schemas, observation and next steps`,body:[`A child throwing objects may be exploring trajectory, not 'being naughty'. A child wrapping dolls in cloth is often enveloping. A child who carries bags of treasures from one area to another is transporting. Name the schema in your observation, then resource it: gutters and balls for trajectory, boxes and lids for enclosing, scarves for enveloping, bags and baskets for transporting, tape and construction for connecting, spinning toys and wheels for rotation.`,`Observation methods (narrative, snapshot, tracking, learning stories) only earn their keep if they change provision. Write what you saw, then interpret separately, then set one next step the team can actually offer tomorrow. Involve the child's interests and the parent's knowledge from home.`,`Sustained shared thinking is the interaction that often follows a good observation: 'You connected all the tubes. I wonder what would happen if this water reached the sand?' Wait. Follow. Do not turn it into a test.`]},{heading:`Inclusive play and non-statutory guidance`,body:[`Inclusive play starts with the environment: height of resources, noise, visual clutter, outdoor access, representation in books and dolls, and time for children who need longer. SEND adaptations might include a quieter entry to role play, a visual sequence for a game, a larger grip on a marker, or an adult who models Makaton for the whole group so the child using it is not isolated.`,`For EAL, keep home language visible, use gesture and real objects, and do not mistake a silent period for delay. For children who have experienced trauma, predictability and a known adult matter more than a dazzling new theme table.`,`Development Matters and Birth to 5 Matters can help a team talk about typical development and curriculum design. They are guidance. Do not write 'DM 3-4 years, not yet' as if it were a statutory grade. The EYFS statutory framework, plus your setting's curriculum, is what inspections and assignments will hold you to.`]}],placementTips:[`Ask your mentor which play type is weakest in the room this week (often construction or outdoor investigative play) and help resource it for three days, then review who used it and how.`,`Complete one narrative observation that names a possible schema, list three resources that would feed it, and share the idea at planning rather than only filing the observation.`,`Practise sustained shared thinking once a session: comment, wait at least ten seconds, and follow the child's idea instead of switching to your planned question.`,`Audit one area for inclusion (can a child who uses a frame, a child with EAL, and a child who hates noise all use it?) and make one physical change.`,`Learn five Makaton signs the room already uses and model them with speech for all children, not only the child with a plan.`,`When you lead an adult-led activity, build in a child-choice moment (which fruit, which book, which route around the garden) so it does not become a lecture.`,`Read the setting's curriculum or planning guidance and note whether it draws on Development Matters, Birth to 5 Matters, or neither. Be able to say how that choice shows in the room.`],quiz:[{id:`po1-q1`,topicId:`po1`,stem:`Which group correctly lists the three EYFS characteristics of effective teaching and learning?`,options:[`Playing and exploring, active learning, and creating and thinking critically.`,`Listening, speaking and writing.`,`Adult-led, adult-initiated and child-led.`,`Prime areas, specific areas and ELGs.`],answer:0,explanation:`The statutory characteristics are playing and exploring, active learning, and creating and thinking critically. They describe how children learn, not the seven areas or the balance of adult/child initiation.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`po1-q2`,topicId:`po1`,stem:`A two-year-old repeatedly fills bags and carries them to the home corner. Which professional interpretation is strongest?`,options:[`The child is stealing resources and should be stopped immediately.`,`The child is not ready for nursery and should return to the baby room.`,`The child is showing a specific learning difficulty that requires an EHCP at once.`,`The child is likely exploring a transporting schema and needs bags, baskets and journeys built into provision.`],answer:3,explanation:`Repeated carrying of objects is a classic transporting schema. The professional response is to resource and extend it, not to pathologise or punish exploratory play.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`po1-q3`,topicId:`po1`,stem:`What is the status of Development Matters and Birth to 5 Matters in England?`,options:[`Both are statutory and settings must choose one as their only curriculum.`,`Both are non-statutory guidance. Settings must meet the EYFS statutory framework and may use either document, both, or their own curriculum.`,`Only Development Matters is legal; Birth to 5 Matters is an Ofsted inspection handbook.`,`They replaced the EYFS in 2024 and the statutory framework no longer applies.`],answer:1,explanation:`This is a frequent exam trap. The EYFS is statutory. Development Matters and Birth to 5 Matters help settings interpret development; they are not law.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`po1-q4`,topicId:`po1`,stem:`Which adult behaviour best illustrates sustained shared thinking?`,options:[`Asking twenty closed questions during a painting activity to check colour names.`,`Telling the child the scientific answer immediately so they do not form a misconception.`,`Sitting with a child at the water tray, wondering together why the boat sinks when filled, and staying with the investigation.`,`Writing a long observation from across the room without speaking to the child.`],answer:2,explanation:`Sustained shared thinking is joint intellectual engagement over time. Closed quizzes, lectures and silent recording are not SST.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`po1-q5`,topicId:`po1`,stem:`Why might a practitioner offer heuristic play rather than a template craft to a group of toddlers?`,options:[`Heuristic play, as described by Goldschmied, supports concentration, sensory investigation and schematic behaviour without a prescribed product, which matches toddler development better than copying an adult model.`,`Template crafts are banned by the EYFS.`,`Heuristic play requires no risk assessment or supervision.`,`Toddlers cannot enjoy adult-led experiences of any kind.`],answer:0,explanation:`The specification expects you to analyse play pedagogy. Heuristic play is developmentally apt for toddlers; that does not mean all adult-led experience is forbidden, nor that it is unsupervised.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`po1-o1`,topicId:`po1`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how a practitioner can use observation of a child's play to plan inclusive next steps. Refer to the characteristics of effective teaching and learning in your answer.`,modelAnswer:`Observation should capture what the child actually does, for how long, with whom, and how they go about it, not only whether a skill looks 'typical'. For example, a narrative note might show that a child returns to the block area, persists when a tower falls (active learning), and tries a wider base after watching a peer (playing and exploring, and the beginnings of critical thinking). That analysis, not a vague label such as 'likes blocks', generates a next step: offer cylinders and photographs of real towers, invite a peer, and plan an adult comment that wonders about stability. Inclusive next steps consider access. If the child has motor difficulty, provide larger, lighter blocks or a raised surface. If the child has EAL, pair the play with key vocabulary and gesture rather than relying on a verbal instruction. Next steps should be small enough to offer in continuous provision the next day, shared with the key person and parent, and reviewed, so that observation is a cycle rather than a folder of unused notes.`,markScheme:[`Accurate reference to observation as factual evidence that informs planning (AO1/AO2).`,`Links at least one characteristic of effective teaching and learning to the example (AO2).`,`Describes a concrete, play-based next step rather than a generic wish (AO2).`,`Includes an inclusive adaptation (SEND, EAL, access or interest) (AO2).`,`Shows that next steps are shared and reviewed, completing the cycle (AO3).`],ao:`AO2`},{id:`po1-o2`,topicId:`po1`,marks:15,command:`Analyse`,stem:`Analyse how a setting can support and promote play, development and early education for a group of three-year-olds, including at least one child with SEND. Evaluate the balance of child-initiated and adult-led experiences.`,scenario:`Willow Room at Hartfield Nursery has 20 children aged 3–4 years, including Jia, who has a hearing impairment and uses speech with Makaton, and Malik, who spends long periods transporting water in bottles between the bathroom and the sand. The room has plentiful table-top crafts with adult models to copy, a small outdoor area used mainly in dry weather, and group times of 25 minutes on the carpet. Several parents have asked for 'more phonics and less playing'. The room leader wants the T Level student to help rethink provision for the next half term.`,modelAnswer:`Play is the statutory vehicle for the EYFS, not a rival to teaching. For three-year-olds, prime areas still dominate: Jia's access to communication will determine her access to literacy and to friends; Malik's transporting is a schematic investigation that can carry mathematics (capacity, emptying and filling), physical development and language if adults resource it instead of stopping it as mess. A room organised around copied crafts and long carpets will under-serve both children and the wider group, because it privileges sitting and product over playing and exploring, active learning, and creating and thinking critically.

Child-initiated play should occupy a substantial part of the day, with continuous provision that is worth choosing: sand and water linked by gutters and bottles for Malik's schema; role play and construction; books and mark-making that include visual support and dual-language texts. Enhancement follows interest, not a new theme every Monday. Adult-led teaching remains essential: a short, well-planned phonics or listening game, a cooking sequence, a story with props. For Jia, adult-led sessions must be visually supported, signed, and short enough that residual hearing fatigue does not look like 'poor behaviour'. The balance is not 50/50 as a slogan; it is a professional judgement that every child meets adult teaching and also has time to apply it in play.

Inclusion is environmental. Whole-group Makaton, a portable sound field or quieter story space, and a key person who checks Jia has understood, stop her being present but excluded. Malik needs journeys that are allowed (a transporting circuit, outdoor water) plus co-regulation if he is stopped without an alternative. Forest School or daily outdoor play in all weathers would give physical challenge and risk-benefit learning that table crafts cannot.

Parents who want 'more phonics' deserve a respectful explanation: systematic teaching will come, and already happens in short bursts, but language, listening and play are the foundations of later reading. Sharing observations of Malik counting bottles, or Jia signing a story, is more persuasive than a lecture on theory. Guidance such as Development Matters can help the team describe progress without turning it into a tick-list.

Evaluation: keeping the current craft-and-carpet model would produce tidy displays and anxious parents in the short term, but would weaken PSED, language and the characteristics of effective learning, and would particularly disadvantage Jia and Malik. Rebalancing towards rich continuous provision, shorter focused teaching, schematic resourcing and consistent AAC is the stronger professional response, provided the team still teach, observe and review rather than drifting into unplanned occupancy of a room.`,markScheme:[`Applies the seven areas and/or characteristics of effective teaching and learning to the scenario (AO2).`,`Analyses play types and/or schemas with reference to Malik and/or Jia (AO3).`,`Evaluates adult-led versus child-initiated balance with a justified judgement (AO3).`,`Includes inclusive adaptations for SEND (and optionally EAL or outdoor access) (AO2).`,`Addresses parent views professionally (AO3).`,`Uses accurate terminology (EYFS, continuous provision, schema, Makaton/AAC, next steps) (AO1).`,`QWC: organised argument, accurate SPAG, professional register.`],ao:`AO3`}]},{id:`po2`,code:`PO2`,title:`Building relationships with children that help them develop`,gist:`PO2 is about the relationships that make development possible. The EYFS key person is a statutory attachment figure in the setting, not a paperwork title. Settling, secondary attachments, PACE, and careful work with children who have experienced trauma or who are looked after all sit here. Professional love is warmth inside boundaries. The child's voice, including UNCRC Article 12, must influence care. Without a secure relationship, even excellent play provision will not be used well.`,mustKnow:[`The key person is a statutory EYFS role: a named practitioner who forms an attachment with the child, settles them, liaises with parents and leads on that child's care and planning.`,`Attachment in the setting is usually a secondary attachment. It still needs sensitivity, consistency and time; children cannot be endlessly rotated between adults.`,`PACE (Playfulness, Acceptance, Curiosity, Empathy) is a connecting stance, especially useful when behaviour communicates distress.`,`Settling-in should be paced, with parents present as needed, transitional objects welcomed, and the key person available at handover. Stopping crying is not the same as being settled.`,`Secondary attachments and a buddy key person protect the child when the key person is on leave, training or off sick.`,`UNCRC Article 12: children have the right to express their views and have them taken seriously. For babies, behaviour and play are the voice.`,`Professional love (Page) is genuine warmth and physical comfort held within professional boundaries, records and handover to parents. It is not ownership of the child and not cold detachment.`,`Children who have experienced trauma or who are looked after need predictability, a consistent adult, and no pressure to recount events. Trauma-informed practice never replaces safeguarding procedures.`,`Self-concept (self-image, self-esteem, ideal self) is shaped by adult responses. Labels, sarcasm and public comparison damage the relationship and the learning.`,`Co-regulation comes before self-regulation. A dysregulated adult cannot offer a secure base.`],sections:[{heading:`The key person and attachment in the setting`,body:[`Bowlby's attachment theory and Ainsworth's observations of sensitivity explain why a child who does not feel safe will not explore. In group care, the key person offers a secure base: greeting the child, doing intimate care, knowing comfort rituals, and being the person a parent can tell about a sleepless night.`,`The role fails when it is only a name on a coat peg. It also fails when a practitioner becomes a rival to the parent. Handover should delight in what the child did and return the child emotionally to the parent. Professional love is the warmth the child needs, bounded by that handover.`,`Secondary attachments are planned, not accidental. A buddy shares enough of the child's care that absence of the key person is a bump, not a cliff. Students support the key person; they should not be given sole responsibility for a child's attachments.`]},{heading:`Settling, PACE and the child's voice`,body:[`Settling-in policies should allow staggered starts, shorter days and a parent staying. Distraction has a place once the child has a relationship; it is not a substitute for presence. Separation anxiety is typical, especially in the first year and at later transitions. Respond to the feeling, keep goodbyes honest and brief, and never slip away without saying goodbye.`,`PACE keeps the relationship intact when behaviour is hard. Playfulness can defuse shame. Acceptance holds the child ('I can see this is too much') without accepting harm to others. Curiosity wonders ('I wonder if the goodbye felt too quick') instead of accusing. Empathy sits with the feeling. PACE does not mean there are no boundaries.`,`Voice of the child is UNCRC Article 12 in practice. Ask, watch, and offer choices in care (which flannel, which book, who changes you). Record preferences and act on them. A toddler who arches away from a hug is communicating; that is as valid as a spoken 'no'.`]},{heading:`Professional love, boundaries and typical behaviour`,body:[`Young children need to be held, soothed and delighted in. Settings that ban all physical contact in the name of safeguarding often increase distress and can themselves be harmful. Safer working means open rooms, informed consent for care, recording, and no secret special relationships. Students follow the setting's intimate-care and physical-contact policies.`,`Typical behaviour shifts with age: babies seek proximity; toddlers test autonomy; two- and three-year-olds often struggle to wait; older children care more about peers and fairness. Interpreting behaviour through development prevents you from treating a tired two-year-old as 'defiant'.`,`Self-efficacy grows when children are offered real choices, time to try, and praise for effort. Doing everything for a child in the name of kindness blocks both skill and self-concept.`]},{heading:`Trauma, looked-after children and transitions`,body:[`Children who have experienced trauma may startle, freeze, control play, or struggle with intimate care and transitions. You do not need the story in order to offer safety. Predictable routines, a consistent adult, warnings before change, and co-regulation are the core offer. Never press a child to talk about home or care history.`,`Looked-after children have a social worker and a care plan. Foster carers need the same quality of handover as any parent, sometimes more, because the child may already have had several placements. Confidentiality about care status is strict: other parents and other children do not need to know.`,`Transitions (starting, moving rooms, starting school, a new baby, family change) can produce clinginess, regression or excitement. Prepare with stories, visits, objects of reference and honest information. Liaise with the receiving key person or teacher so the relationship is handed on, not dropped.`]}],placementTips:[`Learn your key children's comfort rituals (dummy, comforter, song, how they like to be held) from the key person and parent, and use them consistently.`,`At every handover, offer one specific delight ('she spent twenty minutes at the dough with Poppy') before any care information, so the relationship with the parent is not only a list of problems.`,`When the key person is off, follow the buddy plan: be available at arrival, do that child's intimate care if trained and permitted, and do not pass the child around the team.`,`Practise PACE in a difficult moment: name the feeling, stay calm, and wait until the child can take a choice, rather than lecturing while they are still flooded.`,`Offer two genuine choices in a care routine (which book at nap, which sink for handwashing) and notice how voice of the child changes co-operation.`,`If a child is looked after or newly arrived from another setting, ask the mentor what the child already finds comforting, and do not ask the child for their history.`,`Notice your own regulation. If you are rushed or irritated, swap a task with a colleague rather than taking a dysregulated mood into a child's care.`],quiz:[{id:`po2-q1`,topicId:`po2`,stem:`What is the statutory purpose of the key person in the EYFS?`,options:[`To complete all paperwork for a group of children so other staff can play.`,`To replace the parent as the child's primary attachment figure.`,`To form a genuine attachment with the child, support settling, lead on their care and planning, and build the partnership with parents.`,`To supervise students and visitors only.`],answer:2,explanation:`The key person is an attachment role with care, planning and parental partnership attached. It is not a clerical job and it does not replace the parent.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`po2-q2`,topicId:`po2`,stem:`A child who is looked after becomes very distressed when a bank-staff member they do not know starts a nappy change. What is the best PO2 response?`,options:[`Pause, offer a familiar adult to complete or support the care, use the child's known comfort strategies, and avoid pressing them to explain their history.`,`Continue quickly so the child 'gets used to it'.`,`Ask the child, in front of the group, why they are in care.`,`Refuse all intimate care for that child from now on.`],answer:0,explanation:`Trauma-informed, attachment-aware practice uses familiar adults and co-regulation. Interrogation and forced continuation both harm the relationship and may re-trigger distress.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`po2-q3`,topicId:`po2`,stem:`Which statement best captures professional love?`,options:[`Practitioners should avoid warmth so that children do not become confused about who their parents are.`,`Taking a favourite child home at weekends to show commitment.`,`Calling a child 'my baby' on social media to celebrate the bond.`,`Genuine care, delight and physical comfort offered inside professional boundaries, records and handover to the family.`],answer:3,explanation:`Jools Page's professional love is warmth with boundaries. Detachment, blurred home/setting lines and public ownership of the child all fail the concept.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`po2-q4`,topicId:`po2`,stem:`UNCRC Article 12 is best reflected in which everyday practice?`,options:[`Asking a baby nothing, because babies cannot have views.`,`Offering choices, watching body language, and letting a child's preference change a care routine or a play plan.`,`Letting children vote to abolish handwashing.`,`Recording the parent's view only, because adults know best.`],answer:1,explanation:`Article 12 is the right to express views and be taken seriously. For young children that includes behaviour as voice. It does not override safety.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`po2-q5`,topicId:`po2`,stem:`Why is a buddy or secondary key person system professionally important?`,options:[`It saves money on staffing because attachments are interchangeable.`,`It allows the setting to avoid having key persons at all.`,`It provides a planned secondary attachment so that illness, training or leave does not leave the child without a secure base.`,`It is only required for children with an EHCP.`],answer:2,explanation:`Secondary attachments are how settings honour the key-person principle on real working days. They are planned, not a reason to dilute the primary key relationship.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`po2-o1`,topicId:`po2`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how the key person can support a two-year-old who is finding settling-in difficult. Refer to attachment in your answer.`,modelAnswer:`A two-year-old who protests at goodbye is often showing a healthy attachment to their parent, not 'naughtiness'. The key person offers a secondary attachment so the child can use them as a secure base. In practice that means the same adult greeting the child, getting down to their level, and taking the parent’s information about sleep, food and comforters. Settling should be paced: shorter sessions, the parent staying while the child explores, and honest goodbyes rather than slipping out. During the day the key person stays available, offers the comforter, narrates what will happen next, and uses co-regulation if distress rises. PACE helps: accept the feeling, be curious rather than impatient, and keep some playfulness once the child can take it. Handover should tell the parent the truth about the morning and one moment of connection, so the family trusts the relationship. Settling is successful when the child can play and seek the key person if needed, not merely when the crying has stopped.`,markScheme:[`Defines or applies secondary attachment / secure base (AO1/AO2).`,`Describes paced settling and honest goodbyes (AO2).`,`Includes practical key-person strategies (comforter, presence, co-regulation or PACE) (AO2).`,`Refers to partnership with the parent at handover (AO2).`,`Distinguishes stopping crying from actually being settled (AO3).`],ao:`AO2`},{id:`po2-o2`,topicId:`po2`,marks:15,command:`Discuss`,stem:`Discuss how practitioners can develop relationships that facilitate development for children who have experienced disruption, including a looked-after child. Evaluate the balance between professional love and professional boundaries.`,scenario:`Elsie, aged 3 years 8 months, has recently moved to a foster placement and started in Daisies Room (16 children, 3 staff). She watches from the edge of play, startles at sudden noise, and becomes very distressed during nappy changing if her key person, Keisha, is not there. A well-meaning colleague has been asking Elsie questions about 'your old mummy' during snack. Foster carer Dev would like a daily note and a consistent collection routine. Two staff say they are 'keeping a professional distance so she doesn't get confused'.`,modelAnswer:`Elsie's watching, startling and distress in intimate care are coherent responses to disrupted attachment and a new environment. Development will not be facilitated by a distant stance. She needs a secondary attachment that is predictable enough for her to begin to explore. Keisha's role as key person is therefore the intervention: greeting, care, play invitations at Elsie's pace, and a buddy who is already familiar before Keisha's days off. PACE is more useful than interrogation. Curiosity is directed at the here and now ('this room is noisy; we can find a quieter space'), not at her history. The colleague's questions about 'old mummy' should stop at once: they are intrusive, may re-trigger distress, and treat Elsie's life as public material. The DSL or room leader should address that as practice, not as gossip about the family.

Professional love is exactly what Elsie needs: warmth, patience in care, delight when she joins play, a lap if she seeks it, in an open room, recorded, and handed back to Dev at collection. Professional boundaries are what keep that love safe: no secret special relationship, no taking Elsie's story home, no photographs on personal phones, no promises that she will stay with Keisha forever. 'Professional distance' as practised by the two staff is not a boundary; it is an absence of care, and for a looked-after child it can confirm a belief that adults are unreliable. The evaluation is that warmth and boundaries are not opposites. The opposite of professional love is both coldness and blurred roles.

Partnership with Dev is part of the relationship with Elsie. A daily note, consistent collection, and inviting his knowledge of what soothes her are co-production. Transitions should be prepared (visual timetable, warnings, the same peg, the same song). If nappy changing is a flashpoint, two familiar adults, extra time, and as much choice as possible (who, where, which book) honour voice of the child and UNCRC Article 12 without forcing independence before she is ready.

Safeguarding remains live. New unexplained marks, sexualised behaviour, or a disclosure would go to the DSL the same day; relationship-building does not mean holding information. Other children and other parents do not need to know Elsie is looked after. If the team gets the relationship right, play, language and PSED will have a chance to move; if they keep their distance, no amount of enhanced provision will land.`,markScheme:[`Applies attachment / key person / secondary attachment to Elsie (AO2).`,`Discusses trauma-informed responses without graphic detail or interrogation (AO2/AO3).`,`Evaluates professional love against boundaries with a clear judgement (AO3).`,`Addresses the colleague's inappropriate questioning (AO3).`,`Includes foster-carer partnership and confidentiality (AO2).`,`Refers to voice of the child / UNCRC and/or care routines as relationship (AO2).`,`Maintains safeguarding as compatible with warm practice (AO3).`,`QWC: coherent structure, accurate terminology, professional tone.`],ao:`AO3`}]},{id:`po3`,code:`PO3`,title:`Planning, running and reviewing care, play and learning so children make progress`,gist:`PO3 is the planning cycle made real: observe, assess, plan, do, review. You plan for individuals and for the cohort; you treat care routines as learning, not as interruptions; you distinguish continuous, enhanced and adult-led provision; and you check whether what you provided actually made a difference. Parents are part of the review, not an audience for a finished display. Differentiation and scaffolding are how plans become progress for every child.`,mustKnow:[`The planning cycle is observe–assess–plan–do–review (also described as plan–do–review). Missing review is the most common reason provision stagnates.`,`Individual next steps and cohort planning both matter. A room can follow a child's schema and still ensure the group meets curriculum intentions over time.`,`Care routines (mealtimes, nappy changing, toileting, sleep, dressing) are pedagogical opportunities for language, independence, maths, PSED and dignity.`,`Consent, dignity and privacy in care are non-negotiable: tell the child what will happen, offer choices, never display a child, never use care as a punishment.`,`Continuous provision is the core offer; enhanced provision follows interest or next steps; adult-led sessions teach something specific. All three should be planned and reviewed.`,`In-the-moment planning captures current interest; it does not replace a long-term curriculum or an eye on who is being missed.`,`Impact is reviewed through observation, children's views, parent comments and whether next steps have moved. Displays are not evidence of progress.`,`Differentiation adapts the aim, resource, grouping or adult role. Scaffolding is temporary help within that, faded as the child succeeds.`,`Indicators of additional need (language delay, limited play, regression) should trigger the graduated approach, not wait-and-see until school.`],sections:[{heading:`The planning cycle`,body:[`Observation gathers evidence. Assessment interprets it against typical development, the child's own history, and the curriculum. Planning turns that into provision: an environmental change, a resource, an interaction, a care-routine tweak, or a planned activity. Doing is the provision itself, including flexibility when a better opportunity appears. Review asks whether anyone's learning moved, whose interest was missed, and what to try next.`,`Short-term plans (daily or weekly) should be adjustable. Medium-term plans hold projects and coverage. Long-term plans hold the curriculum, values and seasonal or community events. In-the-moment planning sits inside this structure: the adult who joins Malik's water-transporting and later records the mathematics taught is planning, not avoiding planning.`,`Cohort planning looks at patterns: several children not using 1:1 counting, a group avoiding the outdoor climbing, all the three-year-olds needing more turn-taking. Individual next steps sit on top, not instead.`]},{heading:`Care routines as learning`,body:[`A nappy change can include naming body parts respectfully, singing, counting poppers, and waiting for the child to lift their hips. A mealtime can include pouring, choosing, tasting, waiting, and talk. Sleep can include a predictable sequence, a comfort object, and safer-sleeping practice. Dressing can include the language of first/then, left/right, and perseverance.`,`Dignity and consent distinguish education from processing children. Say what you will do. Invite the child to help. Use a private, calm space. Do not discuss the contents of a nappy over the child's head as entertainment. Independence is the aim, but it is scaffolded: you do not leave a two-year-old to fail at the toilet as a character test.`,`For children with SEND or a trauma history, routines may need extra time, the same adult, and visual or object-of-reference cues. That is planning, not indulgence.`]},{heading:`Continuous, enhanced and adult-led provision`,body:[`Continuous provision must be sufficient for the curriculum you claim: if you say you teach mathematics through play, the environment must actually afford counting, pattern, shape and measures every day. Adults still teach inside it.`,`Enhancement is precise: extra funnels because of a schema, a real letter after a grandparent's visit, dual-language labels when a new child starts. Adult-led sessions should be short, intentional and evaluated. If the same adult-led craft happens every Friday regardless of observation, it is a habit, not a plan.`,`Differentiation might mean a visual recipe, a lower easel, a pre-teaching of vocabulary, a smaller group, or an adult who scaffolds the first step then steps back. The aim is participation with stretch, not a simplified ghetto at a separate table forever.`]},{heading:`Reviewing impact and involving parents`,body:[`Review uses the same evidence as assessment: observations, the child's own comments or play, parent notes, and, where relevant, specialist targets. 'The children enjoyed it' is not an evaluation of progress. Ask: who used it, who avoided it, what did they do that they could not do last week, and what will we change?`,`Parents hold evidence you do not have. A two-minute handover, a photo with a caption, a request ('has she been posting objects at home?') and a co-constructed next step are partnership review. For families who do not come to the gate, a phone call, a translated note, or a time that works for them is part of the plan.`,`If review shows a child is not progressing, escalate through the setting's process: key person, SENDCo, graduated approach, and, with parents, other professionals. Planning that never changes is not planning.`]}],placementTips:[`Take one care routine (snack or dressing) and plan a language or independence goal for it; ask your mentor to watch you try it and then review together.`,`Write a next step that can be offered in continuous provision without a new adult-led activity, then check two days later whether the child used it.`,`At planning, ask 'who did we not see in this area last week?' and propose one enhancement aimed at those children.`,`Practise scaffolding: demonstrate once, do it with the child, then stand back. Notice the moment you should have faded the help.`,`Add a parent voice to a review ('Dad said he counted the stairs') so the plan is not setting-only.`,`When you lead an adult-led activity, note two adaptations you actually used and one you would make next time. That is review, not self-criticism.`,`If you see a child struggling in a routine, do not just help them through it; tell the key person so the plan can change.`],quiz:[{id:`po3-q1`,topicId:`po3`,stem:`Which sequence best represents the early years planning cycle?`,options:[`Display, photograph, laminate, file.`,`Observe, assess, plan, do, review.`,`Long-term plan, then ignore observations until the next inspection.`,`Adult-led activity, worksheet, sticker, home.`],answer:1,explanation:`The cycle is a loop. Observation without review, or planning without observation, both fail PO3.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`po3-q2`,topicId:`po3`,stem:`Why are mealtimes treated as educational opportunities in PO3?`,options:[`Because settings are required to teach formal dining etiquette from birth.`,`Because children should not play after they have eaten.`,`Because routines afford language, independence, mathematics, physical skill and PSED when adults plan for dignity, talk and participation rather than processing children quickly.`,`Because Ofsted grades the flavour of the food.`],answer:2,explanation:`Care as learning is a specification theme. The educational value depends on the adult's planning and the child's dignity, not on making lunch into a lesson with a learning objective on the wall.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`po3-q3`,topicId:`po3`,stem:`What is the difference between continuous provision and enhanced provision?`,options:[`Continuous provision is the core, always-available offer; enhanced provision is a planned, temporary change that follows interest or next steps.`,`Enhanced provision is statutory; continuous provision is optional.`,`They are different names for adult-led phonics.`,`Continuous provision is outdoors only; enhancement is indoors only.`],answer:0,explanation:`Accurate terminology is AO1. Enhancement that never changes, or continuous provision that is never taught in, both miss the point.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`po3-q4`,topicId:`po3`,stem:`A review shows that a child with a physical disability has not used the outdoor climbing for a month. What should the team do?`,options:[`Conclude that the child is not interested in physical development.`,`Keep the same provision and write 'not yet' on the tracker until Reception.`,`Ban climbing for everyone so that provision is equal.`,`Treat it as an access and planning issue: adapt the equipment or route, scaffold, involve the child and parent, and review whether participation improves.`],answer:3,explanation:`Review must change provision. Equality as sameness (banning climbing) is not inclusion. Lack of use is evidence about the environment, not only about the child.`,ao:`AO3`},{id:`po3-q5`,topicId:`po3`,stem:`Which example is scaffolding rather than doing the task for the child?`,options:[`Putting the child's coat on fully while they stand still, every day, because it is quicker.`,`Starting the zip, then waiting while the child pulls it up, reducing the help across the week.`,`Telling the child they are lazy for not dressing.`,`Cutting out the entire craft for the child so the display looks neat.`],answer:1,explanation:`Scaffolding is temporary, fading support that keeps the child as the doer. Completing the task for speed or display is not scaffolding.`,ao:`AO2`}],open:[{id:`po3-o1`,topicId:`po3`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how care routines can be planned to promote independence and dignity for children aged 18–36 months.`,modelAnswer:`Routines are repeated, so they are powerful teaching. Independence is planned in small steps: a child fetches their nappy from a low drawer, holds the wipes, or pulls up their own underwear after a prompt. The adult scaffolds, then fades. Dignity means explaining what will happen, using the child's name, offering a choice of who changes them where policy allows, and keeping the child's body private. Language is planned as carefully as a group time: 'first the poppers, then the wipe', which also teaches sequence. Toileting is led by the child's signs of readiness and the family's approach, not by a room-wide deadline. Consent is ongoing; if a child stiffens or says no, the adult pauses, co-regulates, and tries again with more control for the child, unless delay would be unsafe. Review asks whether the child is doing more for themselves than last month, not whether the routine was fast.`,markScheme:[`Treats care as learning, not only as welfare (AO1).`,`Gives concrete independence steps and scaffolding (AO2).`,`Addresses dignity, privacy and/or consent (AO2).`,`Refers to partnership with families or child's readiness (AO2).`,`Includes review of impact (AO3).`],ao:`AO2`},{id:`po3-o2`,topicId:`po3`,marks:15,command:`Evaluate`,stem:`Evaluate how a setting should plan, provide and review opportunities so that both an individual child and the wider cohort make progress. Refer to continuous provision, adult-led teaching and partnership with parents.`,scenario:`In Reception at Oakfield Primary, Miss Hughes and TA Priya plan weekly. Continuous provision is attractive but has not been changed this half term. Adult-led phonics is daily and strong. Theo (4 years 9 months) can blend sounds but avoids writing and still uses a transporting schema outdoors, filling bags with gravel. Several children are not yet using 1:1 counting. Theo's dad rarely attends stay-and-play and says he is 'not a teacher'. A planning file is full of completed sheets; next steps are copied from last month.`,modelAnswer:`The planning cycle in this room is incomplete. Observation of Theo is probably happening informally (everyone can see the gravel bags) but it is not changing provision, and copied next steps show that review is not real. Adult-led phonics is a strength and should stay: Theo's blending shows that focused teaching works for him. The gap is application. Continuous provision that never changes cannot meet a transporting schema or a cohort counting need. Enhancement this week might include clipboards, labels and gravel in trays with numerals, bags and scales outdoors, and a post box so transporting has a literacy purpose. That is individual and cohort planning from the same environmental change.

Adult-led mathematics should be short, practical and then released into play: counting children at the door, setting the snack table, a five-minute game with objects, not another worksheet. Differentiation for Theo might mean accepting mark-making on a tag attached to his bag before expecting a seated writing task. Scaffolding writing could start with his name on a label he attaches to a collection, fading towards more sustained marks. For the children who lack 1:1 correspondence, the adult role in continuous provision is to model tagging objects, not to withdraw them permanently from play for drill.

Parents are part of review. Theo's dad is not a reluctant person; he may feel deskilled by school language. Co-production would use his terms: 'he's a collector; could he count the collection at home and bring it in?' A photograph of Theo using numerals on bags, sent with one sentence, is more respectful than a stay-and-play he cannot attend. Handover at the gate, a phone call, or a weekend photo request may work better than another invitation to sit on a tiny chair in the classroom.

Evaluation: keeping phonics and the static pretty environment would maintain a display-ready classroom and a full file, but it would waste Theo's schema, stall counting for the cohort, and leave next steps as wallpaper. The stronger plan is to treat continuous provision as the main teacher, keep adult-led sessions tight, change the environment in response to evidence, and measure impact by what children do next week that they could not do this week, including evidence from home. That is threshold competence in PO3; a completed pro forma is not.`,markScheme:[`Applies observe–assess–plan–do–review to the scenario (AO2).`,`Evaluates static continuous provision versus enhancement (AO3).`,`Uses Theo's schema and the cohort counting gap as dual planning (AO2/AO3).`,`Keeps adult-led phonics as a strength while arguing for application in play (AO3).`,`Includes differentiation/scaffolding (AO2).`,`Addresses partnership with a parent who feels 'not a teacher' (AO2/AO3).`,`Makes a clear judgement about what should change (AO3).`,`QWC: structured evaluation, accurate terms, professional register.`],ao:`AO3`}]},{id:`po4`,code:`PO4`,title:`Keeping children safe, healthy and well`,gist:`PO4 is the non-negotiable duty to keep children safe while still letting them play, eat, sleep, go online in age-appropriate ways, and feel well. KCSIE and Working Together sit with the EYFS welfare requirements. You must know how to recognise possible abuse at a professional level, report to the DSL, understand LADO and whistleblowing, and manage health (safer sleeping, allergies, medication, oral health, infection). Risk-benefit, not the elimination of all risk, is the play-safety standard. Wellbeing includes mental health and transitions.`,mustKnow:[`Working Together to Safeguard Children (2026) and, in schools, Keeping Children Safe in Education (2026) are the core statutory guidance. The EYFS sets safeguarding and welfare requirements for early years providers. Family Help is the 2026 name for the seamless early-help / s.17 offer.`,`The DSL is the first port of call for a concern. Record factually, same day, including the child's words in quotation marks. Do not investigate, promise confidentiality, or alert an alleged abuser.`,`Section 17 (child in need) and section 47 (reasonable cause to suspect significant harm) are different thresholds. You report; you do not decide the section.`,`LADO co-ordinates allegations against people who work with children. Whistleblowing is how you escalate if the DSL or manager is the problem.`,`Signs of abuse at exam level: unexplained injuries, changes in behaviour, sexualised behaviour not in line with age, neglected physical care, excessive watchfulness, a child who is unusually withdrawn or unusually aggressive. Record and report; do not diagnose a type of abuse as fact.`,`Risk-benefit (Play Safety Forum) keeps challenge in play. Identify hazards, who might be harmed, controls, record, review (HSE five steps).`,`Safer sleeping: back to sleep, clear cot, firm flat surface, appropriate temperature, no smoking, regular checks. Follow NHS and setting policy.`,`Allergies, medication, oral health and the Eatwell Guide are welfare duties. Infection control includes hygiene, exclusion periods and notifiable diseases via the manager and public health advice.`,`Online safety in early years includes setting devices, parent partnerships about what children see at home, and no staff or student use of personal phones with children.`,`Wellbeing covers mental health, co-regulation, and support through transitions. A child who is 'no trouble' can still be struggling.`],sections:[{heading:`Statutory guidance, reporting and allegations`,body:[`If you are worried, tell the DSL the same day. Write what you saw or heard, when, and who else was there. Use the child's words. Opinion can be labelled as opinion ('I was concerned because…') but the record should be usable in a later enquiry. The DSL may take advice from children's social care. You may be asked for your record; you will not usually be told everything that happens next.`,`If a child discloses, listen, stay calm, do not ask leading questions, explain that you will need to tell the DSL so they can be helped, and do not offer a promise you cannot keep. Comfort the child. Then report.`,`Allegations about an adult (including a student) go through the allegations procedure, which includes the LADO. Do not privately 'warn' the person or start your own investigation. If the person you would report to is the subject of the concern, use the deputy, the LADO contact in the policy, Ofsted, or the NSPCC whistleblowing helpline.`]},{heading:`Health, infection and daily welfare`,body:[`Safer sleeping is a daily practice, not a poster. Cots are clear; babies are placed on their backs; checks are recorded; any baby who has rolled is still considered within the policy the setting has written. Never prop a bottle or put a baby to sleep on a play mat as a workaround.`,`Allergy and medication procedures are exact: labelled food, trained staff, care plans, permission, recording. A student must not administer medication unless the setting and provider have agreed it and you have been trained. Oral health means water or milk, limited free sugars, and partnership with families, not shaming a parent about a dummy at the gate.`,`Infection control is handwashing, cleaning, ventilation, and exclusion when policy says so. Notifiable diseases are reported by clinicians; your job is to tell the manager, follow UKHSA advice that comes back, and protect confidentiality about which child is ill.`]},{heading:`Risk-benefit, accidents and online safety`,body:[`Children need to climb, use tools, and experience uncertainty. A good risk assessment makes that possible: surfacing, ratios, teaching safe use, weather plans. Cancelling outdoor play as a default is a wellbeing issue as well as a play issue.`,`When an accident happens: first aid, inform the parent, record, review the assessment if the hazard should have been controlled. Head injuries always go to the parent and follow policy even if the child 'seems fine'.`,`Online safety for under-fives is mostly about adults: setting tablets locked to chosen apps, no YouTube autoplay, no personal devices, and conversations with parents about what children see on family phones. If a child describes sexual or violent content, record and tell the DSL; it may be a safeguarding issue at home as well as an e-safety teaching moment.`]},{heading:`Abuse indicators, Prevent, wellbeing and transitions`,body:[`You are not asked to become a detective. You are asked to notice a child whose presentation changes, whose explanations of injury do not fit, who seems afraid of a person, or whose behaviour in play is significantly out of line with age. Patterns matter as much as single events. Female genital mutilation, forced marriage and fabricated illness are also in the professional landscape for older children; in early years your duty is still to report concerns, not to investigate.`,`Prevent in the early years is proportionate: promote respect and turn-taking, notice concerning language or family views that glorify harm, and report via the DSL. A four-year-old playing superheroes is not a Prevent referral.`,`Wellbeing is promoted through relationships, outdoor time, food, rest, and a climate where feelings can be named. Transitions (room moves, school, family change) should be planned. Mental health in the early years often shows as behaviour, sleep or play; respond with co-regulation and, where needed, the health visitor or other services via the usual partnership routes.`]}],placementTips:[`On day one, identify the DSL and deputy, find the concern form, and ask how quickly a concern must be recorded.`,`Complete a risk-benefit assessment for one outdoor experience using the five HSE steps, including your own safety.`,`Watch a safer-sleeping rest time and check the practice against the written policy (position, clear cot, checks).`,`Learn the allergy children in your room, where the care plans live, and what you must never give at snack.`,`Practise writing a factual accident record (no blame, no diagnosis) and compare it with a model in the setting's file.`,`Never use a personal phone in the room. If you see a colleague doing so, follow the setting's policy; that can be a safeguarding issue.`,`If something worries you about a child or an adult, tell the DSL the same day even if you feel unsure. Unsure and reported is professional; unsure and silent is not.`],quiz:[{id:`po4-q1`,topicId:`po4`,stem:`A child says, 'Daddy hurt my arm.' What should the student do first?`,options:[`Ask detailed questions to find out exactly what happened, then telephone the father for his side of the story.`,`Promise the child it will be a secret if they say more.`,`Wait a week to see if the child says it again, to avoid overreacting.`,`Listen without leading, reassure, explain they will need to tell the DSL, record the child's words, and report the same day.`],answer:3,explanation:`Disclosure procedure is listen, don't lead, don't promise secrecy, record, report. Investigating or delaying are both unsafe.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`po4-q2`,topicId:`po4`,stem:`What is the Play Safety Forum's risk-benefit principle?`,options:[`All outdoor play should be cancelled if there is any possibility of a grazed knee.`,`Only managers may allow children to run.`,`The developmental benefits of an experience should be weighed against likelihood and severity of harm, with proportionate controls, rather than aiming for zero risk.`,`Risk assessments are optional if staffing ratios are met.`],answer:2,explanation:`Risk-benefit is the professional standard for play. Zero risk is neither possible nor desirable for development.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`po4-q3`,topicId:`po4`,stem:`Who co-ordinates the response to an allegation that a member of staff has harmed a child?`,options:[`The local authority designated officer (LADO), via the setting's allegations procedure, usually after the DSL or manager has been informed.`,`The T Level student, as an independent witness.`,`The parent, who should confront the staff member at the gate.`,`Ofsted inspectors on their next routine visit only.`],answer:0,explanation:`LADO is the specialist role for allegations against adults who work with children. Students report; they do not co-ordinate.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`po4-q4`,topicId:`po4`,stem:`Which safer-sleeping practice is aligned with NHS advice in a setting?`,options:[`Placing babies on their tummies so they do not choke, with a pillow and loose blanket.`,`Back to sleep, clear cot, firm flat surface, appropriate temperature, smoke-free environment, and recorded checks.`,`Letting babies sleep in car seats in the room all afternoon because they settle better.`,`Sharing a sleep surface with a baby to promote attachment.`],answer:1,explanation:`NHS safer-sleeping advice is explicit. Prone sleep, pillows, car seats as prolonged sleep spaces and co-sleeping in a setting are not acceptable practice.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`po4-q5`,topicId:`po4`,stem:`A four-year-old in superhero play shouts, 'You're the baddie, I'm going to smash you.' What is the most proportionate response?`,options:[`An immediate Prevent referral, because the language is violent.`,`Ignore all aggressive play because imagination must never be limited.`,`Exclude the child from the setting for promoting terrorism.`,`Stay with the play, teach that bodies are not for hurting, offer limits and alternative language, and only report via the DSL if there is a wider pattern of concerning language, family views or behaviour that goes beyond typical play.`],answer:3,explanation:`Prevent in early years is real but proportionate. Typical superhero play is not a referral. A pattern of concerning extremist language or behaviour would go to the DSL.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`po4-o1`,topicId:`po4`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how a practitioner should respond if they have a safeguarding concern about a child, including how the record should be written.`,modelAnswer:`The practitioner should tell the DSL or deputy the same day, even if they are not certain. They should not investigate, ask leading questions, promise the child secrecy, or contact parents if that might increase risk. The record should be made promptly, dated and signed, and should stick to facts: what was seen or heard, when, where, and who was present. The child's words should appear in quotation marks. Interpretation can be noted as such ('I was concerned because the explanation did not match the bruise') but labels such as 'this is definitely physical abuse' are for the investigation, not for the first record. The record is stored securely according to policy, not in a personal notebook that leaves the building. If the DSL is implicated, the practitioner uses the whistleblowing or allegations route, including the LADO contact in the policy.`,markScheme:[`Reports to DSL same day; no independent investigation (AO1/AO2).`,`Describes factual recording, child's words, date (AO2).`,`Avoids leading questions and false promises (AO2).`,`Mentions secure storage and/or whistleblowing/LADO if the DSL is the issue (AO2/AO3).`],ao:`AO2`},{id:`po4-o2`,topicId:`po4`,marks:15,command:`Analyse`,stem:`Analyse how a setting can safeguard children while still promoting play, health and wellbeing. Evaluate the difference between removing risk and managing it.`,scenario:`Sunflower Room (0–2s, 12 children, 4 staff) has had two incidents this month: a baby found with a loose muslin in the cot at check time, and a toddler who needed first aid after slipping on water beside an indoor water tray. A parent has emailed to demand that water play and outdoor climbing are stopped. Staff are anxious. The manager has also reminded the team that a member of bank staff was seen using a personal phone in the baby room. You are the student asked to contribute to the team discussion.`,modelAnswer:`These incidents are different kinds of problem and should not produce the same response. A loose muslin in a cot is a safer-sleeping failure: it is a known SIDS-related hazard, it has little or no play benefit in that context, and the control is to keep cots clear, follow NHS advice, and record checks. That is a risk to be eliminated, not balanced. The bank staff member's personal phone is a safeguarding and image-consent failure: personal devices do not belong in the baby room. That also requires a hard rule, induction, and, if it continues, the allegations or disciplinary route, not a risk-benefit conversation.

Water play and climbing are different. They offer physical development, scientific thinking, sensory regulation and the chance to learn about slippery floors and height. The Play Safety Forum position is that children need challenge. The HSE five steps would identify the hazard (wet floor, fall from a frame), who might be harmed (toddlers, a baby on the floor, staff backs), and proportionate controls: towels and a designated splash zone, immediate drying, a lower tray, close supervision, surfacing and teaching feet-first climbing, rather than a ban. The parent's fear is understandable and deserves a calm explanation, not dismissal and not automatic surrender of the curriculum.

Health and wellbeing sit beside safety. Cancelling outdoor play would reduce accidents in the short term and increase poor physical development, sleep problems and limited risk-judgement in the long term. Infection control, allergy management and oral health should remain exact while play remains ambitious. Staff anxiety is itself a wellbeing issue: a team that is frightened will either over-restrict or under-supervise. Reviewing the two incidents, retraining on safer sleep, restating the phone rule, and rewriting the water-play assessment together is how you restore professional confidence.

Evaluation: treating every hazard as a reason to stop an experience would make the room quieter and poorer. Treating every hazard as an acceptable price of play would be negligent. Threshold competence is discrimination: eliminate hazards with no developmental case (unsafe sleep, personal phones), and manage hazards that come with worthwhile play. That analysis should be written, shared with parents, and reviewed after the next incident or near miss, not only after an inspection.`,markScheme:[`Distinguishes safer-sleeping and personal-phone issues from play hazards (AO3).`,`Applies HSE steps and/or risk-benefit / Play Safety Forum (AO2).`,`Analyses parent pressure without abandoning professional standards (AO3).`,`Includes reporting/DSL/LADO or policy routes where relevant to the phone (AO2).`,`Connects outdoor play and challenge to wellbeing and development (AO2).`,`Clear evaluation of eliminate versus manage (AO3).`,`QWC: reasoned argument, accurate terminology, no graphic content.`],ao:`AO3`}]},{id:`po5`,code:`PO5`,title:`Working with colleagues, families and other professionals to support children's development`,gist:`PO5 is how the rest of the specialism actually gets done: nobody improves a child's development alone. Partnership is two-way and, at its best, co-produced with families. Information is shared within UK GDPR and, when a child is at risk, despite it. Multi-agency work (health visitor, SALT, portage, social worker, SENDCo) needs a Team Around the Child, not a stack of unconnected plans. Disagreement is handled professionally. Confidentiality is need-to-know, not secrecy that blocks help.`,mustKnow:[`Two-way communication means parents and carers contribute knowledge, not only receive newsletters. Co-production means they help set and review outcomes.`,`UK GDPR governs photographs, journals, addresses and health data. Safeguarding information can be shared without consent where necessary to protect a child; that is lawful, not casual.`,`Need-to-know is the working rule: share what the cook, the key person or the social worker requires to do their job; do not brief the whole staff room.`,`Multi-agency partners commonly include the health visitor, speech and language therapist, portage worker, social worker, educational psychologist, GP, SENDCo and, where relevant, the LADO.`,`The Team Around the Child (or family) brings those people into one plan with the parents. The key person often attends and must prepare accurate, factual updates.`,`Professional disagreement is expected. Use the setting's escalation or resolving-professional-differences process; do not undermine a colleague in front of a parent or a child.`,`Barriers to partnership include shift patterns, language, previous poor experience of services, poverty, distrust, and professional jargon. The setting has to adapt, not blame the family.`,`Confidentiality is a duty to the child. It is not a reason to withhold a safeguarding concern from the DSL.`],sections:[{heading:`Two-way communication and co-production`,body:[`Handover, learning journals, phone calls, interpreters, home visits (where used), and parents' evenings are tools. The test is whether information travels both ways. 'How did she sleep?' is as important as 'she ate well'. Co-production in SEND or early help means the parent helps write the outcome ('we want him to sit at family meals without distress') rather than signing a plan written in education-speak.`,`Hard-to-reach is usually a description of the service, not the family. Offer times that match shifts, translated summaries, a quiet space, and a named person. Do not discuss a child at the gate in a crowd. Do not use a child as the messenger for difficult news.`,`The child's voice belongs in the partnership. Even a two-year-old can show who they want involved and what they enjoy. Record it and take it to the meeting.`]},{heading:`Information sharing, GDPR and safeguarding override`,body:[`Ask: is sharing necessary, proportionate, relevant, accurate, timely and secure? Record what was shared, with whom, and why. Parents are usually told when information is passed to another service, unless telling them would increase risk to the child.`,`Photographs, observations and medical details do not go on personal phones or personal cloud drives. Students follow the setting's device policy exactly.`,`A colleague who wants 'the full story' out of curiosity is not a lawful recipient. Need-to-know protects dignity and trust. Conversely, refusing to tell the DSL because 'the parent asked me to keep it secret' is a misunderstanding of confidentiality.`]},{heading:`Multi-agency working and the TAC`,body:[`Health visitors hold the two-year review and are often the first partner when development or home conditions raise a question. SALT leaves strategies that only work if the room uses them every day. Portage works through the parent in the home; the setting should ask what to mirror. Social workers co-ordinate child-in-need or child-protection plans; the setting's reports must be factual and prompt. The SENDCo joins the threads inside the setting.`,`A TAC meeting needs a clear purpose, the right people, the parent's presence or view, and actions with names and dates. The student may contribute observations but does not lead the meeting. Follow up: a plan that nobody reads is not partnership.`,`Roles can overlap and contradict (a therapist wants a quiet space; the curriculum wants outdoor play). The professional task is to integrate, not to pick a tribe.`]},{heading:`Disagreement, boundaries and professional courtesy`,body:[`Disagreeing with a parent about dummy use, diet or behaviour is common. Start with their goal for the child, share the setting's evidence, and look for a trial they can accept. Lecturing at the gate destroys the relationship the child needs you to have.`,`Disagreeing with a colleague (about an assessment, a next step, or whether to refer) should happen away from children, using questions and evidence. If a child's safety is at issue, escalate the same day rather than waiting for a comfortable moment.`,`Professional boundaries with families include not becoming a personal friend, not babysitting on the side without declaration, not using personal social media, and not giving out a personal number unless policy allows a work device. Warmth and partnership do not require those crossings.`]}],placementTips:[`Prepare one handover a day that includes a question to the parent, not only a report, and note what you learn.`,`Ask to read (and then contribute to) a report for another professional, checking that every sentence is factual and necessary.`,`Find out how the setting books an interpreter and when it last did so. If a family is receiving everything in English only, raise it with your mentor.`,`Sit in on a planning or TAC discussion if invited, and write a three-bullet summary of actions, owners and dates.`,`When you disagree with a planned next step, practise a professional question: 'How will this work for Aisha given what SALT asked us to do?'`,`Never discuss a child in the staff-room doorway, the car park or a group chat. If you hear it happening, do not add to it; take a concern to the mentor or DSL.`,`Keep a list of who is who (SENDCo, DSL, health visitor contact route) in your placement folder, without storing children's personal data in it.`],quiz:[{id:`po5-q1`,topicId:`po5`,stem:`A parent asks you not to tell anyone that a social worker visited. The child has unexplained bruises. What should you do?`,options:[`Keep the secret, because GDPR forbids all sharing without consent.`,`Post a coded message in the staff WhatsApp group.`,`Thank the parent for telling you, explain that you must share safeguarding information with the DSL, and report the same day.`,`Wait until the parent puts the request in writing.`],answer:2,explanation:`Safeguarding overrides a request for secrecy. GDPR is not a barrier to sharing to protect a child. The DSL is the correct route, not a group chat.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`po5-q2`,topicId:`po5`,stem:`What is the main purpose of a Team Around the Child meeting?`,options:[`To allow every agency to read out their own unconnected plan without discussion.`,`To replace the key person.`,`To decide on exclusion from the setting.`,`To create a single, co-ordinated plan with the family and the professionals who actually work with the child.`],answer:3,explanation:`TAC is co-ordination and co-production, not a series of monologues or a disciplinary hearing.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`po5-q3`,topicId:`po5`,stem:`Which example is need-to-know information sharing rather than gossip?`,options:[`Telling the whole staff room why a family is involved with social care.`,`Telling the cook and the staff who serve snack about a child's allergy, and telling the key person about a new safeguarding plan, but not discussing either in public.`,`A student repeating a parent's medical history to friends at college, without names, as a 'case study'.`,`Leaving a printed family assessment on the photocopier.`],answer:1,explanation:`Need-to-know is functional: people who must act get the information they need. Anecdotes, public discussion and careless documents all fail PO5.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`po5-q4`,topicId:`po5`,stem:`A SALT programme asks staff to reduce questions and model language. A colleague says, 'We haven't got time for that.' What is the professional response?`,options:[`Raise the issue with the colleague and SENDCo, explain that daily modelling is the intervention, and agree how the room will use the strategy, escalating if the child's progress is blocked.`,`Ignore the SALT advice, because room staff know the child better.`,`Tell the parent that the colleague is incompetent.`,`Photocopy the SALT report and pin it on the parent noticeboard.`],answer:0,explanation:`Partnership with other professionals only works if strategies are used. Challenge is in-house and child-centred; public complaint and noticeboard breaches are not.`,ao:`AO3`},{id:`po5-q5`,topicId:`po5`,stem:`Why might a family be described as 'hard to reach', and what should the setting do?`,options:[`It usually means the family is uninterested; the setting should stop trying after one missed meeting.`,`It is a legal category that allows the setting to exclude the child.`,`Barriers often sit in the service (times, language, jargon, previous distrust). The setting should adapt communication, offer interpreters, and keep inviting partnership without shaming.`,`It means the child must be referred to social care automatically.`],answer:2,explanation:`PO5 treats partnership barriers as something the setting works on. Automatic referral or giving up both misunderstand the role.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`po5-o1`,topicId:`po5`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how a key person should work with a speech and language therapist and a parent to support a child with delayed language.`,modelAnswer:`The key person holds the daily relationship, so they are the person who makes a SALT programme real. Before the appointment they gather factual observations (what the child says, in what contexts, whether home language is used, what the parent already notices). In the meeting they listen to the targets, ask how those will look in play and care routines, and check the parent has understood without jargon. After the visit the key person models the agreed strategy (for example, commenting instead of quizzing, using two-word models, pairing Makaton with speech) and shares the same approach with colleagues so the child does not meet a different method in every area. With the parent, they co-produce one or two home strategies that fit family life, not a homework packet. Review is joint: a short note to SALT and to the parent about what has changed. Confidentiality means the diagnosis is not discussed at the gate with other families.`,markScheme:[`Roles of key person, SALT and parent are distinct and complementary (AO1/AO2).`,`Daily modelling of strategies, not waiting for weekly therapy, is explained (AO2).`,`Co-production with the parent is included (AO2).`,`Review and confidentiality are mentioned (AO2/AO3).`],ao:`AO2`},{id:`po5-o2`,topicId:`po5`,marks:15,command:`Discuss`,stem:`Discuss how a setting should work in partnership when a family is reluctant to engage and several agencies are involved. Evaluate how confidentiality and information sharing should be handled.`,scenario:`Amira, aged 2 years 4 months, attends Buttercup Room. Health visitor notes at the two-year review flagged delayed language and poor weight gain. A social worker is completing a child-in-need assessment (section 17). Mum, Yasmin, works nights, misses meetings, and told the student, 'I'm sick of people writing about us.' An interpreter has never been offered, although Yasmin's English is limited. A colleague wants to 'fill everyone in' at staff meeting about the family's housing. SALT has sent a programme that is not yet in use. Yasmin has asked that the social worker not be told Amira arrived hungry twice this week.`,modelAnswer:`Yasmin's reluctance is information, not a character flaw. Night shifts, no interpreter, and a trail of professionals 'writing about us' are barriers the setting can reduce. Partnership starts with a named key person who meets Yasmin at a time she can do, with an interpreter, and who asks what she wants for Amira. Co-production would take her goal (perhaps that people stop judging her) and connect it to Amira's language and nutrition, rather than presenting a list of failures. Written communication should be short, translated, and two-way.

Multi-agency work needs a TAC, not separate conversations that Yasmin has to repeat. The health visitor, social worker, SALT, key person and Yasmin should share one plan: who is supporting feeding, who is modelling language, when it will be reviewed. The SALT programme must be used in the room; that is the setting's part of the contract. The student contributes observations and does not lead the agencies.

Information sharing is the evaluation point. UK GDPR does not block safeguarding or child-in-need work. Amira arriving hungry is welfare information the social worker needs for a section 17 assessment; the key person should explain to Yasmin, with an interpreter, that this will be shared because it is about Amira's needs, and then share it factually with the DSL and, as agreed, with the social worker. It is not gossip. By contrast, 'filling everyone in' at staff meeting about housing is not need-to-know. The cook may need allergy and snack information; the whole team needs only what they must do for Amira that day. Housing detail in an open meeting would confirm Yasmin's belief that people write about her and would be a confidentiality breach.

Professional disagreement may arise: Yasmin does not want sharing; the duty is to Amira. Handle that with honesty, not secrecy and not humiliation. If staff disagree about whether the threshold is now child protection rather than child in need, they escalate through the DSL, not through corridor votes. The judgement is that respectful partnership and lawful sharing are the same professional task: tell Yasmin as much as safety allows, share with those who must act, and stop the staff-room story. That is how Amira's development, not the adults' curiosity, stays at the centre.`,markScheme:[`Analyses barriers (shift, language, distrust) and setting adaptations (AO3).`,`Applies TAC / multi-agency roles including SALT and social worker (AO2).`,`Evaluates GDPR versus safeguarding/child-in-need sharing of the hunger information (AO3).`,`Applies need-to-know to the staff-meeting gossip (AO3).`,`Keeps the child's needs central while treating the parent with respect (AO3).`,`Student role is appropriate (contribute, do not lead, report via DSL) (AO2).`,`QWC: organised discussion, accurate legal/professional terms, non-judgemental register.`],ao:`AO3`}]}];export{e as t};